plied with \\(4\\).\n\nUpon simplification, it achieves maximum product of\nall values. And therefore, final correct answer should relate \nit correctly with\n\n[asy]\ndraw((0, 1)--(9, 1),linewidth(1));\ndraw((3, 6)--(6, 6),linewidth(1));\nlabel(\"9\",(0, 1),S);\nlabel(\"10\",(3, 6),E);\ndraw((5, 7)--(5, 8)--(6, 8),linewidth(1,dashgraphics));\nlabel(\"8\",(5, 8),E);   \ndraw((18, 9)--(27, 9),linewidth(1));\ndraw((21, 12)--(24, 12),linewidth(1));\nlabel(\"27\",(21, 12),SW);\nlabel(\"35\",(24, 12),SW);\ndraw((36, 9)--(45, 9),linewidth(1));\ndraw((39, 12)--(42, 12),linewidth(1));\nlabel(\"42\",(39, 12),NE);\nlabel(\"52\",(42, 12),NE);\ndraw((44, 12)--(44, 21)--(45, 21),linewidth(1));\nlabel(\"39\",(44, 21),NE);\nlabel(\"51\",(45, 21),N); draw(graph(2x+1, 1, 5),linewidth(1));\ndraw((5, -4)--(5, 20),linewidth(1));\ndraw(graph(5x-1, 5, 10),linewidth(1));\ndraw((5, -9)--(5, 16),linewidth(1));\ndraw(interp(graph(5x-8, 5, 10), graph(5x+2, 5, 10), 5)--interp(graph(5x-4, 5, 10), graph(5x+12, 5, 10), 5),linewidth(1));\ndraw(interp(graph(5x-2, 5, 10), graph(5x+7, 5, 10), 5)--interp(graph(5x-7, 5, 10), graph(5x+14, 5, 10), 5),linewidth(1));",
